{"type":"WebSearch","query":""Proposition 44" "71%" OR "25%" OR "likely voters" 2026","content":"**California Proposition 44 (November 3, 2026 ballot) is an initiated state statute requiring certain nonprofit Federally Qualified Health Centers (community clinics serving medically underserved areas) to spend at least 90% of their annual revenue on “program services” (primarily patient care and related charitable activities), with penalties for shortfalls.** It is also known as the Clinic Funding Accountability and Transparency Act. A yes vote would impose the 90% spending floor (with the Attorney General setting rules and possible waivers); a no vote would leave existing nonprofit and federal rules in place. Fiscal impact is estimated as increased state costs in the low tens of millions of dollars per year, covered by fees. It was sponsored by SEIU-United Healthcare Workers West (via Californians for Responsible Healthcare) and qualified via petition signatures. Opponents include the American Academy of Pediatrics CA, California Medical Association, California Primary Care Association, Planned Parenthood Affiliates of California, and others. Both major state parties have opposed it.[[1]](https://quickguidetoprops.sos.ca.gov/propositions/2026-11-03/44)

The numbers in your query match recent polling of **likely voters**. The Public Policy Institute of California (PPIC) Statewide Survey (October 16–22, 2026; 1,086 likely voters) found **25% yes / 71% no / 4% undecided** on Prop 44. This was a drop from the September PPIC poll (34% yes / 61% no). Opposition is broad, with 60%+ no across partisan, demographic, and regional groups. Other coverage and forecasts (e.g., On Point Politics) treat it as very likely to fail, with a polling average around 29% yes and near-zero chance of passage. Prediction markets also showed low odds (around 8–11%).[[2]](https://www.ppic.org/publication/ppic-statewide-survey-californians-and-their-government-october-2026/)

This is one of 14 statewide propositions on the 2026 California ballot. For official details, see the California Secretary of State voter guide or Ballotpedia. Polling can shift, and results are not yet final as of the latest available data.","citations":["https://quickguidetoprops.sos.ca.gov/propositions/2026-11-03/44","https://www.ppic.org/publication/ppic-statewide-survey-californians-and-their-government-october-2026/"],"allowed_domains":null}

{"type":"WebSearch","query":"California Proposition 44 election results November 3 2026 yes no votes","content":"**Unofficial results for California Proposition 44 (November 3, 2026 General Election):** Yes 1,475,928 votes (82.0%), No 324,029 votes (18.0%). It passed.[[1]](https://electionresults.sos.ca.gov/returns/ballot-measures/county)

These are unofficial election night figures from the California Secretary of State, with 99.8% of precincts (23,810 of 23,863) reporting. Results are scheduled to be certified by December 11, 2026.[[1]](https://electionresults.sos.ca.gov/returns/ballot-measures/county)

Proposition 44 is an initiative statute requiring certain private nonprofit community health clinics (federally qualified health centers) to spend at least 90% of annual revenue on program services (with penalties for shortfalls). A yes vote supported the requirement; a no vote opposed it.","citations":["https://electionresults.sos.ca.gov/returns/ballot-measures/county"],"allowed_domains":null}

{"type":"WebSearch","query":"site:electionresults.sos.ca.gov Proposition 44","content":"**Proposition 44** is a statewide ballot measure (initiative statute) on the November 3, 2026, California General Election ballot. It would require certain private nonprofit Federally Qualified Health Centers (community clinics serving medically underserved areas) to spend at least 90% of their annual revenue on “program services” that advance their charitable purpose, including patient care. Spending on other costs (such as administration) would be limited to 10% or less. Clinics falling short would face penalties equal to the shortfall (refundable if they comply within 5 years); the state Attorney General would issue guidance on qualifying expenses, with possible waivers in exceptional cases. False reporting could lead to criminal charges. The Legislative Analyst estimates increased state enforcement costs in the low tens of millions of dollars per year, covered by fees on the clinics.[[1]](https://voterguide.sos.ca.gov/propositions/44/title-summary.htm)

The official election results site (electionresults.sos.ca.gov) currently shows **no votes reported** for Proposition 44 (or any 2026 ballot measures). As of the latest update (November 2, 2026, around 6:28 p.m.), 0 of 23,860 precincts are reporting, with 0 yes votes (0.0%) and 0 no votes (0.0%). Results will become available after 8:00 p.m. on Election Night (November 3, 2026) and will be unofficial until certified (expected by December 11, 2026). Vote-by-mail, provisional, and other ballots will continue to be counted afterward. You can check the live statewide and county-by-county results, maps, and reporting status directly on the site.[[2]](https://electionresults.sos.ca.gov/returns/ballot-measures/county)

For full details, including the official title/summary, analysis, arguments/rebuttals, and proposed law text, see the California Secretary of State’s Official Voter Information Guide. Supporters argue it directs more healthcare dollars to patient care and increases accountability/transparency. Opponents (including medical associations) warn it could cut clinic funding, force operating losses, reduce services, or cause closures. No official supporters were listed in some summaries; opponents include groups like the American Academy of Pediatrics California and California Medical Association. Campaign finance data is also available via related SOS pages.","citations":["https://voterguide.sos.ca.gov/propositions/44/title-summary.htm","https://electionresults.sos.ca.gov/returns/ballot-measures/county"],"allowed_domains":null}
